    <section xml:id="sec-stat-review">
        <title>Stats Review</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                This is intended to be only a brief review of some concepts from
                stats.
                If you need a more thorough refresher, please use your stats
                book or other online resources.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Sampling distribution</title>

            <p>
                The objective of statistical inference is to draw conclusions
                about the population using a sample from that population.
            </p>

            <p>
                A <term>statistic</term> is any quantity whose value can be
                calculated from sample data.
                Before the data is obtained, we are uncertain about the value
                that the statistic will assume.
                Therefore, a statistic is a random variable.
                In particular, a sample mean <m>\bar{X}</m> has a probability
                distribution.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Hypothesis testing</title>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to know if exposure to asbestos is associated
                with lung disease.
            </p>

            <p>
                Procedure:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Take some rats and randomly divide them into two groups:
                        <ul>
                            <li>Group 1: expose to asbestos</li>
                            <li>Group 2: leave unexposed</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    Compare the disease rate in the two groups.
                    Consider the following two hypotheses:
                    <md>
                        H_0: \text{The disease rate is the same in the two groups}
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        H_1: \text{The disease rate is not the same in the two groups}
                    </md>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Only use hypothesis testing to test well-defined
                        hypotheses.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In hypothesis testing, we assume <m>H_0</m> is true
                        unless there is strong evidence to reject <m>H_0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <!-- <li>
                    <p>
                        There are two types of errors we can make:
                        <table>
                            <tabular>
                                <row>
                                    <cell/>
                                    <cell>Retain null</cell>
                                    <cell>Reject null</cell>
                                </row>

                                <row>
                                    <cell><m>H_0</m> true</cell>
                                    <cell>✓</cell>
                                    <cell>Type I error</cell>
                                </row>

                                <row>
                                    <cell><m>H_1</m> true</cell>
                                    <cell>Type II error</cell>
                                    <cell>✓</cell>
                                </row>
                            </tabular>
                        </table>
                        where
                        <md>
                            \alpha = P(\text{type I error})
      = P(\text{reject } H_0 \mid \text{\(H_0\) is true}),
                        </md>
                        <md>
                            \beta = P(\text{type II error})
      = P(\text{fail to reject } H_0 \mid \text{\(H_0\) is false}).
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </li> -->

                <li>
                    We assume that the samples are drawn at random from two
                    independent normal populations.
                    <image source="figures/twoNormalDistributions.png">
                        <shortdescription>
                            <p>
                                Two side-by-side normal distributions with means
                                mu 1 and mu 2 and variances sigma 1 squared and
                                sigma 2 squared
                            </p>
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </li>

                <!-- <li>
                    The power of the test is defined as
                    <md>
                        \text{Power} = 1 - \beta
      = P(\text{reject } H_0 \mid \text{\(H_0\) is false}).
                    </md>
                </li> -->

                <li>
                    A hypothesis of the form <m>\theta = \theta_0</m> is called
                    a simple hypothesis.
                </li>

                <!-- <li>
                    A hypothesis of the form <m>\theta &gt; \theta_0</m> or
                    <m>\theta &lt; \theta_0</m> is called a compound hypothesis.
                </li> -->

                <li>
                    A test of the form <m>H_0: \theta = \theta_0</m> versus
                    <m>H_1: \theta \ne \theta_0</m> is called a two-sided test.
                </li>

                <!-- <li>
                    A test of the form
                    <md>
                        H_0: \theta \leq \theta_0 \text{ versus } H_1: \theta &gt; \theta_0
                    </md>
                    or
                    <md>
                        H_0: \theta \geq \theta_0 \text{ versus } H_1: \theta &lt; \theta_0
                    </md>
                    is called a one-sided test.
                </li> -->

                <li>The most common tests are two-sided.</li>
                <!-- <li>
                    <p>
                        To test a hypothesis:
                        <ol>
                            <li>Collect a random sample.</li>
                            <li>Compute an appropriate test statistic.</li>
                            <li>
                                Reject or retain the null hypothesis based on
                                the calculated statistic.
                                This involves specifying the probability of a
                                Type I error, <m>\alpha</m>, called the
                                significance level of the test.
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li> -->
            </ul>

            <p>
                Suppose that we have two treatments with samples taken for each
                treatment such that:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The experiment was performed using a completely
                        randomized design.
                        This means that:
                        <ol>
                            <li>The population was randomly sampled.</li>
                            <li>The data are normally distributed.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    The two treatments come from two populations with identical
                    variance.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                Depending on whether we know the variance or not, we will use
                the <m>Z</m>-test and the <m>t</m>-test, respectively.
                For our class, we will be working only with the <m>t</m>-test.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title><m>t</m>-test</title>

            <p>
                In practice, we often do not know the variance.
                In that case, the test statistic is
            </p>
            <md>
                t_0 =
  \frac{\overline{y}_1-\overline{y}_2}
  {S_p\sqrt{\frac{1}{n_1}+\frac{1}{n_2}}},
            </md>

            <p>
                where <m>S_p\sqrt{\frac{1}{n_1}+\frac{1}{n_2}}</m> is called the
                standard error of the difference in the means, abbreviated as
                <m>se(\overline{y}_1-\overline{y}_2)</m>, and
            </p>
            <md>
                S_p^2 =
  \frac{(n_1-1)S_1^2+(n_2-1)S_2^2}
  {n_1+n_2-2}.
            </md>

            <p>
                To determine whether to reject <m>H_0: \mu_1=\mu_2</m>, compare
                <m>t_0</m> to the <m>t</m>-distribution with <m>n_1+n_2-2</m>
                degrees of freedom (DOF):
            </p>
            <md>
                \text{If } \lvert t_0\rvert &gt; t_{\alpha/2,\,n_1+n_2-2},
  \text{ reject } H_0.
            </md>

            <p>
                This procedure is called
                <term>the two-sample <m>t</m>-test</term>.
            </p>

            <!-- <ol>
                <li>
                    For a one-sided hypothesis, such as
                    <m>H_0: \mu_1\leq\mu_2</m> versus
                    <m>H_1: \mu_1 &gt; \mu_2</m>:
                    <md>
                        \text{If } t_0 &gt; t_{\alpha,\,n_1+n_2-2}, \text{ reject } H_0.
                    </md>
                </li>

                <li>
                    For a one-sided hypothesis, such as
                    <m>H_0: \mu_1\geq\mu_2</m> versus
                    <m>H_1: \mu_1 &lt; \mu_2</m>:
                    <md>
                        \text{If } t_0 &lt; -t_{\alpha,\,n_1+n_2-2}, \text{ reject } H_0.
                    </md>
                </li>
            </ol> -->

            <example>
                <title>Portland cement formulation experiment</title>

                <statement>
                <p>
                    Tension bond strength data is given fo the Portland cement
                    formulation experiment are given below. (Source: Design and Analysis of Experiments, D.C. Montgomery, 8th edition.)
                </p>
                    <p>
                        Formulate the null hypothesis that the two formulations yield the same tension bond strength. Then, compute the corresponding <m>t</m>-statistic. 
                    </p>
                                        <p>
                        Choosing <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, we would reject <m>H_0</m>
                        if <m>t_0 &gt; t_{0.025,18}=2.101</m>, or if
                        <m>t_0 &lt; -t_{0.025,18}=-2.101</m>. Do we reject <m>H_0</m> in this case? What is the corresponding conclusion?
                    </p>

                <table>
               <title>Tension strength for modified and unmodified mortar samples.</title>
               
               
                    <tabular>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>j</m></cell>
                            <cell>Modified mortar</cell>
                            <cell>Unmodified mortar</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>16.85</cell>
                            <cell>16.62</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>2</cell>
                            <cell>16.40</cell>
                            <cell>16.75</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                            <cell>17.21</cell>
                            <cell>17.37</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>4</cell>
                            <cell>16.35</cell>
                            <cell>17.12</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>5</cell>
                            <cell>16.52</cell>
                            <cell>16.98</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>6</cell>
                            <cell>17.04</cell>
                            <cell>16.87</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>7</cell>
                            <cell>16.96</cell>
                            <cell>17.34</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>8</cell>
                            <cell>17.15</cell>
                            <cell>17.02</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>9</cell>
                            <cell>16.59</cell>
                            <cell>17.08</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>10</cell>
                            <cell>16.57</cell>
                            <cell>17.27</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>

                <table>
                <title>mean, variance, standard deviation, and number of samples for the modified and unmodified mortar.</title>
                    <tabular>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Modified mortar</cell>
                            <cell>Unmodified mortar</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>
                                <m>\overline{y}_1=16.76\text{ kgf/cm}^2</m>
                            </cell>
                            <cell>
                                <m>\overline{y}_2=17.04\text{ kgf/cm}^2</m>
                            </cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell><m>S_1^2=0.100</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>S_2^2=0.061</m></cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell><m>S_1=0.316</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>S_2=0.248</m></cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell><m>n_1=10</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>n_2=10</m></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <!-- <p>
                        Since the standard deviations are reasonably similar, we
                        conclude that the samples came from populations with
                        equal variances.                        
                    </p> -->
                    <p>
                        The test hypotheses are
                    </p>
                    <md>
                        H_0: \mu_1=\mu_2
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        H_1: \mu_1\ne\mu_2.
                    </md>


                    <md>
                        S_p^2 =
      \frac{(n_1-1)S_1^2+(n_2-1)S_2^2}
      {n_1+n_2-2}
      =0.081,
                    </md>

                    <p>
                        so <m>S_p=0.284</m>.
                        The test statistic is
                    </p>
                    <md>
                        t_0 =
      \frac{\overline{y}_1-\overline{y}_2}
      {S_p\sqrt{\frac{1}{n_1}+\frac{1}{n_2}}}
      =-2.20.
                    </md>

                    <p>
                        Because <m>t_0=-2.20 &lt; -t_{0.025,18}=-2.101</m>,
                        reject <m>H_0</m> and conclude that the mean tension
                        bond strengths of the two formulations are different.
                    </p>

                    <figure xml:id="portland-mortar-rejection-region">
                        <image source="figures/portlandMortar_rejectionRegion.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                Rejection region for the Portland Mortar data
                                set.
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                        <caption>
                            Portland mortar example, rejection region.
                        </caption>
                    </figure>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>The use of <m>p</m>-values in hypothesis testing</title>

            <!-- <p>
                <term>Fixed significance level testing:</term> The result of the
                hypothesis test is reported at a specified level of significance
                (the <m>\alpha</m>-value).
                One disadvantage of fixed significance level testing is that it
                conveys no idea about the location of the test statistic within
                the rejection region.
            </p>

            <p>
                Reporting “reject <m>H_0</m> ” or “retain <m>H_0</m> ” is not
                very informative.
                Instead, we ask the question: for the test statistic that I
                have, at what level <m>\alpha</m> will we reject <m>H_0</m>?
                Note that if <m>H_0</m> is rejected at some level <m>\alpha</m>,
                it will also be rejected at any level <m>\alpha'</m> where
                <m>\alpha' &gt; \alpha</m>.
            </p> -->

            <p>
                <term><m>p</m>-value:</term> The smallest <m>\alpha</m> value at
                which <m>H_0</m> is rejected.
            </p>

            <!-- <figure xml:id="p-value">
                <image source="figures/pValue.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A two-panel figure demonstrating fixed-significance
                        level testing (left) versus using the p value (right).
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
                <caption>
                    Fixed-significance level testing (left) versus using the
                    <m>p</m>-value (right).
                </caption>
            </figure> -->

            <p>
                The <m>p</m>-value is a measure of the evidence against
                <m>H_0</m>: the smaller the <m>p</m>-value, the stronger the
                evidence against <m>H_0</m>.
                Usually, the following scale is used:
            </p>

            <table>
          
            
                <tabular>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>p</m>-value</cell>
                        <cell>Evidence</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>p &lt; 0.01</m></cell>
                        <cell>Very strong evidence against <m>H_0</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>0.01\text{-}0.05</m></cell>
                        <cell>Strong evidence against <m>H_0</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>0.05\text{-}0.10</m></cell>
                        <cell>Weak evidence against <m>H_0</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>p &gt; 0.1</m></cell>
                        <cell>Little or no evidence against <m>H_0</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <figure xml:id="p-value-reject">
                <image source="figures/pvalueReject.png" width="40%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Rejection criteria using a set p-value shown as a
                        horizontal function with two levels: accept and reject
                        with the transition occurring at the p value.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Confidence intervals</title>

            <p>
                We are often interested in knowing how much the means differ
                instead of only whether they differ.
                The interval within which the parameters would be expected to
                lie is called a confidence interval.
            </p>

            <p>
                To obtain an interval estimate of <m>\theta</m>, we need to find
                two statistics <m>L</m> and <m>U</m> such that the probability
                statement
            </p>
            <md>
                P(L \leq \theta \leq U) = 1-\alpha
            </md>

            <p>
                is true.
                The interval <m>L \leq \theta \leq U</m> is called a
                <m>100(1-\alpha)</m> percent confidence interval for the
                parameter <m>\theta</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Interpretation of the confidence interval: A <m>95\%</m>
                confidence level implies that <m>95\%</m> of all samples would
                give an interval that includes whatever parameter is being
                estimated.
                Specifically, if we take a large number of samples, and for each
                one construct a confidence interval (CI) of the form
                <m>L \leq \theta \leq U</m>, then in the long run, with
                <m>95\%</m> confidence for example, the constructed interval
                will contain the parameter <m>\theta</m> <m>95\%</m> of the
                time.
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore, the confidence interval is not so much a statement
                about any specific realization of the CI; instead, it pertains
                to what would happen if a very large number of like intervals
                were constructed using the same formula.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="confidence-interval-interpretation">
                <image source="figures/CI.png" width="45%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Eleven realizations of the confidence interval on
                        separate lines and one vertical line showing the true
                        mean mu.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
                <caption>Interpretation of the confidence interval.</caption>
            </figure>

            <!-- <example>
                <title>Cast-part length</title>

                <p>
                    In a sample of <m>n=31</m>, the length of a cast part was
                    found to have an average of <m>\overline{x}=80</m> cm.
                    Assuming that the preferred height is normally distributed
                    with <m>r=2</m> cm, obtain a confidence interval for
                    <m>\mu</m>, the true average of the population.
                </p>
            </example> -->
        </paragraphs>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-Assessing-linReg-coefficients-estimates">
        <title>Assessing Coefficient Estimate Accuracy</title>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Bias in estimation</title>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 50%">
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Assume a true value <m>\mu^*</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            An estimate from training data is
                            <m>\widehat{\mu}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The estimate is unbiased if
                            <m>E(\widehat{\mu})=\mu^*</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The sample mean is unbiased for the population mean:
                            <md>
                                E(\widehat{\mu}) =
          E\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_i X_i\right) =
          \mu.
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The standard variance estimate is biased
                            <note>
                                <p>
                                    (underestimation)
                                </p>
                            </note>:
                            <md>
                                E(\widehat{\sigma}^2) =
          E\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_i
          (X_i-\overline{X})^2\right]
          \neq \sigma^2.
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </sidebyside>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Linear regression is unbiased</title>

            <sidebyside widths="28% 32% 40%">
                <figure>
                    <image source="figures/Fig3-3a.png">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Scatter plot of simulated data with linear
                            relationship plus noise with overalid true line
                            relationship and least squares linear fit.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <figure>
                    <image source="figures/Fig3-3b.png">
                        <shortdescription>
                            10 linear fits for the same data with 10 different
                            noise realizations.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            100 data points drawn from <m>Y=2+3X+\epsilon</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\epsilon</m> is drawn from a normal distribution
                            with mean <m>0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The red line is the true relationship; the blue line
                            is the least-squares estimate.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Repeat this 10 times and plot all the resulting
                            lines in variations of blue.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The resulting models are slightly different, but all
                            lie around the true relationship shown in red.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </sidebyside>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Variance in estimation</title>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 55%">
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            True value <m>\mu^*</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Estimate from training data <m>\widehat{\mu}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Variance of the sample mean:
                            <md>
          \operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\mu}) =
          \operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\mu})^2 =
          \frac{\sigma^2}{n}.
        </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Standard error measures by how much a sample statistic, e.g, <m>\hat{\mu}</m> differs from the true population value, e.g., <m>\mu</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The more data you have, the smaller the variance and
                            the better the estimate.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </sidebyside>
            <note>
                <title>Standard error versus standard variation</title>
                <p><term>Standard deviation</term> measures the amount of spread in a sample from the mean.<line/>
                <term>Standard error</term> measures the statistical accuracy of a sample statistic (like a mean), i.e., (how much it deviates from the true population mean).
                </p>
            </note>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Variance of linear regression estimates</title>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Variance of linear regression estimates:
                            <m>
        \operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\beta}_0)^2
        =
        \sigma^2\left[
        \frac{1}{n}
        +
        \frac{\overline{x}^2}
        {\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\overline{x})^2}
        \right]
      </m>
                            <m>
        \operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\beta}_1)^2
        =
        \frac{\sigma^2}
        {\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\overline{x})^2},
      </m>
                            where <m>\sigma^2=\operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <aside>
    <p>If data are more spread out, the standard error is smaller.</p>
  </aside>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The residual standard error is an estimate of
                            <m>\sigma</m>: <m>RSE=\sqrt{RSS/(n-2)}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Confidence Interval</title>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
                <p>
                    The <m>95\%</m> confidence interval for <m>\beta_1</m>
                    approximately takes the form
                    <m>
      \widehat{\beta}_1 \mathbin{\pm}
      2\cdot\operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\beta}_1).
    </m>
                    <note>
                        <p>
                            The same form works for <m>\beta_0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </note>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <term>Interpretation:</term> There is approximately a
                    <m>95\%</m> chance that the interval
                    <m>
      \left[
      \widehat{\beta}_1-2\cdot\operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\beta}_1),
      \widehat{\beta}_1+2\cdot\operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\beta}_1)
      \right]
    </m>
                    will contain <m>\beta_1</m> when we repeatedly approximate
                    <m>\widehat{\beta}_1</m> using repeated samples.
                </p>
            </sidebyside>

            <example>
                <title>CI in Advertising Data</title>

                <sidebyside widths="55% 40%">
                    <figure>
                        <image source="figures/Fig3-1.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                scatter plot with linear fit and residuals
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                    <div>
                        <p>
                            For the advertising data set, the <m>95\%</m>
                            confidence intervals are:
                        </p>

                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\beta_1: [0.042, 0.053]</m>
                                </p>

                                <ul>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            First line through <m>[0,8]</m> and
                                            <m>[300,20.6]</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>

                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            Second line through <m>[0,7]</m> and
                                            <m>[300,21.9]</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\beta_0: [6.130, 7.935]</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </sidebyside>
            </example>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Hypothesis testing</title>

            <sidebyside widths="50% 40%">
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>H_0:</m> There is no relationship between
                            <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m>.
                            <aside><p>Null hypothesis.</p></aside>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>H_1:</m> There is some relationship between
                            <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m>.
                            <aside><p>Alternative hypothesis.</p></aside>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <aside>
                    <m>
                        H_0: \beta_1=0
                    </m>
                    <line/>
                    <m>
                        H_A: \beta_1\neq0
                    </m>

                    <p>
                        Since, if <m>\beta_1=0</m>, the model reduces to
                        <m>Y=\beta_0+\epsilon</m>, <m>X</m> is not associated
                        with <m>Y</m>.
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </sidebyside>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Test statistic and <m>p</m>-value</title>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
                <p>
                    Test statistic:
                    <md>
                        t=\frac{\widehat{\beta}_1-0}
          {\operatorname{SE}(\widehat{\beta}_1)}.
                    </md>
                    <m>t</m>-distribution with <m>n-2</m> degrees of freedom.
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Student_t_pdf-wikipedia.png" width="70%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        student t-distribution for four different values of nu.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>

            <note>
                <p>
                    A small <m>p</m>-value indicates that it is unlikely to
                    observe such a substantial association between the predictor
                    and response by chance, in the absence of a real association
                    between them.
                </p>
            </note>

            <example>
                <title>Advertising example</title>



                <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
                    <figure>
                        <image source="figures/Table3-1.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                linear fit table for the sales versus TV
                                advertising in 1000 dollars data.
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                    </figure>

                    <figure>
                        <image source="figures/Fig3-1.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                scatter plot with linear fit and residuals
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>
            </example>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>
                Assessing the accuracy of the module: Residual Standard Error
                (RSE)
            </title>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 45%" valign="top">
                <stack>
                    <p>
                        RSE
                        <md>
                            RSE = \sqrt{\frac{RSS}{n-2}}
                        </md>
                        <md>
                            = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n-2}\sum_i (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </stack>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Estimate of the standard deviation of
                            <m>\epsilon</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Average amount that the response will deviate from
                            the true regression line.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </sidebyside>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Assessing the accuracy of the module: $R^2$</title>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 45%" valign="top">
                <stack>
                    <p>
                        <emphasis>R squared:</emphasis>
                        <md>
                            R^2 = \frac{TSS-RSS}{TSS} = 1-\frac{RSS}{TSS}
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        where total sum of squares is
                        <md>
                            TSS = \sum_i (y_i - \overline{y})^2
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </stack>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            TSS is total variance in the response <m>Y</m>,
                            variability before regression.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            RSS is the amount of variability after the
                            regression.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>R^2</m> is the proportion of variability in
                            <m>Y</m> that can be explained using <m>X</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Close to 1: a large proportion of variability is
                            explained by the regression.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Close to 0: the regression does not explain much of
                            the variability in the response.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </sidebyside>

            <example>
                <title>
                    <p>
                        Advertising example
                    </p>
                </title>

                <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" valign="top">
                    <stack>
                        <image source="figures/Advertising-SalesVsTV.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                Sales versus TV advertising scatter plot with
                                linear fit line.
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                        <p>
                        <md>
                            R^2 = 0.61
                        </md>
                        </p>
                    </stack>

                    <stack>
                        <image source="figures/Advertising-SalesVsRadio.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                Sales versus radio advertising scatter plot with
                                linear fit line.
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                        <p>
                        <md>
                            R^2 = 0.33
                        </md>
                        </p>
                    </stack>

                    <stack>
                        <image source="figures/Advertising-SalesVsNewspaper.png">
                            <shortdescription>
                                Sales versus newspaper advertising scatter plot
                                with linear fit line.
                            </shortdescription>
                        </image>
                        <p>
                        <md>
                            R^2 = 0.05
                        </md>
                        </p>
                    </stack>
                </sidebyside>
            </example>
            <exercise>
                <title>TSS and RSS</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        For the shown data, answer the following questions
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="day05-TSS-RSS-task1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Sketch the deviation from the mean <m>y_i-\bar{y}</m> for each data point.
                        </p>
                        <image source="figures/TSS_RSS_example.png" width="70%">
                            <shortdescription>Five data points roughly following a linear trend overlaid with their mean line a linear regression line.</shortdescription>                            
                        </image>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task  xml:id="day05-TSS-RSS-task2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Sketch the residual <m>y_i-\hat{y}_i</m> for each data point.
                        </p>
                        <image source="figures/TSS_RSS_example.png" width="70%">
                            <shortdescription>Five data points roughly following a linear trend overlaid with their mean line a linear regression line.</shortdescription>                            
                        </image>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="day05-dev-from-mean">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            If the data points are given by <md>(1,1.7), (2,4.9), (3,4.1), (4,7.8), (5,7.5)</md>. Find the lengths of the bars you sketched in <xref ref="day05-TSS-RSS-task1"/>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Use the answer to <xref ref="day05-dev-from-mean"/> to find the TSS.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="day05-RSS-pieces">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Assume that the linear regression line is given by <m>\bar{y}=0.85+1.45x</m> and using the data points given in <xref ref="day05-dev-from-mean"/>, find the lengths of bars you sketched in <xref ref="day05-TSS-RSS-task2"/>. 
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                 <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Use the answer to <xref ref="day05-RSS-pieces"/> to find the RSS.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                
            </exercise>
        </paragraphs>
    </section>
    <p>
        Group work: Work in groups to complete <xref ref="activities" text="custom">Day05 in-class activity</xref>. 
    </p>
